A result can be sound without being major, and new without being important. The paper earns its strongest interest from excluding an entire class of finite adaptive modal BV algorithms, not merely a particular Fourier construction. It makes oracle and readout assumptions inspectable and shows why a characteristic-two linearization changes the familiar BV situation. Those are reasonable grounds for a short theoretical note.

The principal significance risk is that a specialist may view the result as a short corollary of standard amplitude-degree and modal-discrimination facts. The response should not be more peripheral examples. It should be a crisp theorem, an explicit comparison to the strongest antecedent, and one useful generalization or explanatory example if it genuinely strengthens the contribution.

